00:12:53.090 --> 00:12:55.010
You can go live.

00:12:55.010 --> 00:12:56.890
And it's really good money,

00:12:56.890 --> 00:12:58.230
and they're not even there,

00:12:58.230 --> 00:13:01.023
they're another country
away most of the time.

00:13:03.890 --> 00:13:06.190
Like yesterday, for instance,

00:13:06.190 --> 00:13:09.960
I was on there maybe
25 minutes, 26 minutes,

00:13:09.960 --> 00:13:12.060
I think it was actually
'cause it times you

00:13:12.060 --> 00:13:14.833
on your phone or device,
whatever you're using.

00:13:16.284 --> 00:13:17.617
And I made $114.

00:13:18.920 --> 00:13:21.470
Like when I got done
calculating the bids on there.

00:13:24.880 --> 00:13:27.500
But yeah, there is definitely
a lack of opportunity

00:13:27.500 --> 00:13:28.470
of any kind of jobs.

00:13:28.470 --> 00:13:31.660
I started in the medical field, actually.

00:13:31.660 --> 00:13:34.493
I started in the medical field actually.

00:13:35.970 --> 00:13:40.523
Let's see, I started out, I
graduated high school at 15.

00:13:41.480 --> 00:13:43.610
So I had to go to vocational school

00:13:43.610 --> 00:13:46.870
to get extra credits for
a nurse aide license.

00:13:46.870 --> 00:13:48.490
Well, I seen in the newspaper

00:13:48.490 --> 00:13:51.770
they were having the class start

00:13:51.770 --> 00:13:53.463
up at the nursing home in Hyden.

00:13:55.740 --> 00:14:00.040
Well, I done it there, and I
took my state test in Hazard.

00:14:00.040 --> 00:14:01.230
I got my CNA license.

00:14:01.230 --> 00:14:02.965
I worked my hours on.

00:14:02.965 --> 00:14:04.083
I had got all my credits.

00:14:05.734 --> 00:14:09.447
And then I went advanced
into school, got my LPNs.

00:14:10.880 --> 00:14:13.423
You know, a nurse practitioner.

00:14:14.540 --> 00:14:17.973
That's whenever I
discovered pain medicine.

00:14:19.140 --> 00:14:20.410
I mean, I knew of it of course

00:14:20.410 --> 00:14:23.173
because my mom had been
there so long on it.

00:14:25.420 --> 00:14:26.900
That's when I really discovered it.

00:14:26.900 --> 00:14:28.550
And I just had to take a step back.

00:14:28.550 --> 00:14:30.560
I looked at myself one day
and I said, you know what?

00:14:30.560 --> 00:14:31.780
This is awful.

00:14:31.780 --> 00:14:34.200
Because I mean, I loved that job.

00:14:34.200 --> 00:14:35.033
I did.

00:14:35.033 --> 00:14:38.570
You have to have a heart
for helping, for real,

00:14:38.570 --> 00:14:40.630
to have a job like that.

00:14:40.630 --> 00:14:42.420
And it's something sort of comes back

00:14:42.420 --> 00:14:44.890
to me being good-hearted like that

00:14:45.771 --> 00:14:48.070
is from me doing the things that

00:14:48.070 --> 00:14:51.260
had really actually happened to me

00:14:51.260 --> 00:14:53.883
that I regretted and I hated my mom.

00:14:54.720 --> 00:14:57.623
I don't hate her and I respect my mom.

00:15:01.123 --> 00:15:05.457
That's just the way that I
was raised by her parents.

00:15:06.390 --> 00:15:08.913
I do go by a set of
morals, I like to think.

00:15:11.187 --> 00:15:12.520
I don't know.

00:15:12.520 --> 00:15:14.330
I'm a queer human being really.

00:15:14.330 --> 00:15:16.460
- [Mark] Do you have any regrets?

00:15:16.460 --> 00:15:17.780
- All kinds of regrets.

00:15:17.780 --> 00:15:22.023
I regret not staying at the nursing home,

00:15:23.942 --> 00:15:27.050
and actually going on
and getting my orients

00:15:27.050 --> 00:15:29.480
especially now with the
world being the way it is.

00:15:29.480 --> 00:15:34.010
And down here, like they
lack of medical technology,

00:15:34.010 --> 00:15:35.830
medical staff, all the time.

00:15:35.830 --> 00:15:37.660
I remember even having to go in

00:15:37.660 --> 00:15:41.540
and work 16 and 18 hour shifts.

00:15:41.540 --> 00:15:44.433
I had just had a baby, my first kid.

00:15:47.871 --> 00:15:52.200
So I kind of regret not
staying there a little bit.

00:15:52.200 --> 00:15:55.730
But like I said, that's where
I dabbled into pain medicine.

00:15:55.730 --> 00:15:58.593
That's when I developed my
pain pill habit or whatever.

00:15:59.610 --> 00:16:01.380
- [Mark] Do you ever consider leaving

00:16:01.380 --> 00:16:02.930
this part of the country?

00:16:02.930 --> 00:16:03.810
- All the time.

00:16:03.810 --> 00:16:05.270
- [Mark] Taking your kids somewhere

00:16:05.270 --> 00:16:06.280
where they might have a future?

00:16:06.280 --> 00:16:07.113
- All the time.

00:16:07.113 --> 00:16:09.103
But the thing about that is,

00:16:11.210 --> 00:16:16.210
I'm not real comfortable
with new things, maybe?

00:16:16.350 --> 00:16:18.160
I don't know how you
would say that, change?

00:16:18.160 --> 00:16:19.650
I'm not comfortable with that.

00:16:19.650 --> 00:16:22.720
I'm not comfortable with
meeting new people at all.

00:16:22.720 --> 00:16:24.815
I guess because of the things

00:16:24.815 --> 00:16:28.343
that me and my mom's been through.

00:16:30.370 --> 00:16:34.870
I've got all kinds of mental health issues

00:16:34.870 --> 00:16:36.613
on account of all that.

00:16:38.956 --> 00:16:41.180
And I still try to try to tell myself

00:16:41.180 --> 00:16:44.300
that it was okay, look at me now.

00:16:44.300 --> 00:16:45.760
She's done good for me.

00:16:45.760 --> 00:16:48.710
And that's kinda messed up that
I even would think that way.

00:16:48.710 --> 00:16:53.710
But yeah, I've actually not
just taking me and my kids, no.

00:16:54.418 --> 00:16:58.050
No, I've actually just thought
about going and getting up,

00:16:58.050 --> 00:17:00.740
packing me a bag that I can pack,

00:17:00.740 --> 00:17:03.580
and just walk it off
and never looking back.

00:17:03.580 --> 00:17:05.007
- [Mark] What about your kids?

00:17:05.007 --> 00:17:07.570
- I know that my kids are took care of.

00:17:07.570 --> 00:17:09.380
My little girl, her dad.

00:17:09.380 --> 00:17:11.340
I couldn't ask for no better father.

00:17:11.340 --> 00:17:13.943
My son, he's six.

00:17:16.230 --> 00:17:17.293
He's six.

00:17:19.410 --> 00:17:23.180
His dad don't really have
anything to do with him at all.

00:17:23.180 --> 00:17:26.550
So he's sorta the man
of the house, actually.

00:17:26.550 --> 00:17:29.483
He has been with my mom,
because I had to go to jail.

00:17:30.410 --> 00:17:33.980
He was 18 months old when I come home.

00:17:33.980 --> 00:17:37.870
I think he was three
months old when I left.

00:17:37.870 --> 00:17:41.140
So he was 18 months old
when I come home from jail.

00:17:41.140 --> 00:17:43.040
So he's been with my mom a little bit.

00:17:43.900 --> 00:17:46.880
That's probably why I don't
have as much hate for her now.

00:17:46.880 --> 00:17:48.883
I've sort of let things go about it,

00:17:49.760 --> 00:17:52.300
about the way she done
me when I was growing up.

00:17:52.300 --> 00:17:53.560
She was pretty abusive too.

00:17:53.560 --> 00:17:55.733
Gosh, she still is a little abusive.

00:17:56.670 --> 00:18:00.150
But anyway, yeah, I know that my kids

00:18:00.150 --> 00:18:01.330
are well tooken care of.

00:18:01.330 --> 00:18:04.700
I just, I can't be the mom
that they need, you know?

00:18:04.700 --> 00:18:07.033
And I talk to them about things like that.

00:18:08.600 --> 00:18:11.680
I don't whoop my kids,
I don't discipline them.

00:18:11.680 --> 00:18:15.270
I'll let their nanny, which is my mom,

00:18:15.270 --> 00:18:18.393
or my little girl's dad do it.

00:18:19.550 --> 00:18:22.680
On account of where she was abusive.

00:18:22.680 --> 00:18:26.110
Because, I've got another little boy,

00:18:26.110 --> 00:18:29.540
and that's another regret is him.

00:18:29.540 --> 00:18:31.460
He was just born.

00:18:31.460 --> 00:18:33.580
I was doing real good in a clinic

00:18:33.580 --> 00:18:35.053
to keep me off pain pills.

00:18:36.100 --> 00:18:38.930
He was crying one night,

00:18:38.930 --> 00:18:41.200
and listen this went on
for two, three weeks.

00:18:41.200 --> 00:18:46.053
Me and my sister took him to
every emergency room around.

00:18:47.620 --> 00:18:52.620
And so it was Laurel, Perry,
Manchester, and then Hyden.

00:18:52.900 --> 00:18:55.593
We took him like eight different times.

00:18:56.431 --> 00:18:58.620
So we took him twice on everyone of them.

00:18:58.620 --> 00:19:01.370
Over in Perry County, we
took him to the pediatrician

00:19:01.370 --> 00:19:04.560
and that's when we found
out he had pyloric stenosis,

00:19:04.560 --> 00:19:06.440
and he needed immediate surgery.

00:19:06.440 --> 00:19:09.080
But what I was getting at
is I remember that baby.

00:19:09.080 --> 00:19:13.460
Me and my sister had walked him
so much, and I love my kids.

00:19:13.460 --> 00:19:15.190
That's why my kids are
placed where they're at,

00:19:15.190 --> 00:19:17.023
because I can't provide for them.

00:19:20.100 --> 00:19:21.743
I'm not adult enough.

00:19:22.840 --> 00:19:24.330
You know what I'm trying to get at?

00:19:24.330 --> 00:19:26.220
I'm not really sure what I'm trying to.

00:19:26.220 --> 00:19:27.510
I can't put it in words, but.

00:19:27.510 --> 00:19:28.343
- [Mark] How old are you?

00:19:28.343 --> 00:19:29.724
- Huh?

00:19:29.724 --> 00:19:30.557
- [Mark] How old are you?

00:19:30.557 --> 00:19:32.353
- I'm 30, I just turned 30 in September.

00:19:34.240 --> 00:19:37.110
But yeah, I remember me and
my sister took time about,

00:19:37.110 --> 00:19:39.610
and every time Colson would eat a bottle,

00:19:39.610 --> 00:19:41.300
he would vomit it right back up.

00:19:41.300 --> 00:19:43.890
So I was getting aggravated, you know.

00:19:43.890 --> 00:19:47.520
He wasn't probably a month
old and I remember one night,

00:19:47.520 --> 00:19:49.520
'cause I told you, my mom was abusive.

00:19:49.520 --> 00:19:51.420
This is one of the most regrets I have

00:19:51.420 --> 00:19:53.183
in my life is letting my baby go.

00:19:56.141 --> 00:19:58.910
I remember 'cause my mom,
again, she was abusive

00:19:58.910 --> 00:20:00.990
and that's why I don't discipline my kids.

00:20:00.990 --> 00:20:02.581
I don't.

00:20:02.581 --> 00:20:03.480
So when I'm around them, I can tell,

00:20:03.480 --> 00:20:05.020
I get real short-tempered with them.

00:20:05.020 --> 00:20:06.070
You know what I mean?

00:20:06.925 --> 00:20:07.770
So I don't whoop them

00:20:07.770 --> 00:20:09.300
because I'm afraid that I couldn't stop.

00:20:09.300 --> 00:20:10.705
I'm afraid.

00:20:10.705 --> 00:20:11.690
Because I see her come out at me

00:20:11.690 --> 00:20:14.570
every freaking day, I hate that.

00:20:14.570 --> 00:20:15.410
Honestly.

00:20:15.410 --> 00:20:18.400
Again, like I said, I
don't hate my mom at all.

00:20:18.400 --> 00:20:19.560
I respect her.

00:20:19.560 --> 00:20:20.990
It's my mother.

00:20:20.990 --> 00:20:21.870
She put me in this world.

00:20:21.870 --> 00:20:23.200
She can definitely take me out.

00:20:23.200 --> 00:20:25.460
And she's always told me that.

00:20:25.460 --> 00:20:26.963
I'm a firm believer of that.

00:20:29.617 --> 00:20:33.410
The night that I had tried
so hard to figure out

00:20:33.410 --> 00:20:35.238
what was wrong with my baby, you know?

00:20:35.238 --> 00:20:37.500
I felt like I let everybody down

00:20:37.500 --> 00:20:40.450
because I couldn't get him to stop crying.

00:20:40.450 --> 00:20:41.710
He cried for three days straight.

00:20:41.710 --> 00:20:42.960
My sister couldn't.

00:20:42.960 --> 00:20:45.280
We scrubbed puke up, dah dah dah dah dah.

00:20:45.280 --> 00:20:48.060
I just remember, and like right now,

00:20:48.060 --> 00:20:50.310
in my stomach, me telling you all this,

00:20:50.310 --> 00:20:51.923
I don't speak on it a whole lot.

00:20:53.820 --> 00:20:57.610
I remember holding his little
arms and just squeezing him.

00:20:57.610 --> 00:20:59.910
And then I remember coming to,

00:20:59.910 --> 00:21:01.690
and I had to just walk away.

00:21:01.690 --> 00:21:03.690
I laid him down on a blanket on the floor

00:21:03.690 --> 00:21:07.450
and the doctors just acted
like it was actually normal.

00:21:07.450 --> 00:21:11.920
And I was thinking to
myself, that's not normal.

00:21:11.920 --> 00:21:12.753
You know?

00:21:12.753 --> 00:21:14.953
Yes, it was normal when I was growing up

00:21:14.953 --> 00:21:17.540
because that was all I was used to.

00:21:17.540 --> 00:21:19.033
That's not normal.

00:21:24.214 --> 00:21:27.220
So eventually I gave him to his dad.

00:21:29.240 --> 00:21:31.703
That was his first son,
his only child actually.

00:21:32.890 --> 00:21:34.380
Which I can go see him and stuff.

00:21:34.380 --> 00:21:35.780
I just feel like it's easier

00:21:36.980 --> 00:21:40.000
on emotional attachment
and all that stuff.

00:21:40.000 --> 00:21:41.800
That's something I really do regret.

00:21:42.842 --> 00:21:46.740
I regret not being in any
of the three of my kids'

00:21:46.740 --> 00:21:50.080
life a lot more, because I know how it is

00:21:50.080 --> 00:21:51.567
not having a mom and dad.

00:21:51.567 --> 00:21:54.070
And how it is having to raise myself.

00:21:54.070 --> 00:21:57.503
And actually my six year
old, he's pretty independent.

00:21:59.560 --> 00:22:02.210
It hurts seeing him be
independent like that

00:22:02.210 --> 00:22:05.360
because I know that he's just a kid.

00:22:05.360 --> 00:22:08.219
He should be allowed to have a childhood.

00:22:08.219 --> 00:22:09.290
You know, I didn't get to do that.

00:22:09.290 --> 00:22:11.190
I had to grow up really, really quick.

00:22:12.100 --> 00:22:13.496
- [Mark] What's the most important lesson

00:22:13.496 --> 00:22:14.663
you've learned in your life?

00:22:19.220 --> 00:22:21.063
- My most important lesson is.

00:22:23.440 --> 00:22:25.900
It's not really the life that you live.

00:22:25.900 --> 00:22:28.880
It's the life that you
will choose for yourself.

00:22:28.880 --> 00:22:32.542
So, and I'm just now
actually learning that.

00:22:32.542 --> 00:22:33.788
Like I said, I'm 30.

00:22:33.788 --> 00:22:35.820
Listen, I was depressed
when I turned 30 this year,

00:22:35.820 --> 00:22:39.380
back in September, but
I realized I'm naughty.

00:22:39.380 --> 00:22:41.330
I'm still a child, you know,

00:22:41.330 --> 00:22:43.093
because I didn't get to have a kid.

00:22:44.490 --> 00:22:48.960
So the most important lesson
is don't never fall in.

00:22:50.270 --> 00:22:51.780
They say history repeats itself,

00:22:51.780 --> 00:22:53.923
but don't never fall into that cycle.

00:22:55.814 --> 00:22:57.980
Don't listen to what other people says,

00:22:57.980 --> 00:23:00.210
listen to what your own
mind tells you to do.

00:23:00.210 --> 00:23:01.043
You know?

00:23:01.043 --> 00:23:03.733
Just listen to your heart, I guess.

00:23:04.990 --> 00:23:05.823
- [Mark] All right.

00:23:05.823 --> 00:23:06.656
Well, Ashlyn, thank you so much

00:23:06.656 --> 00:23:10.010
for sharing your story,
and good luck with the rest

00:23:10.010 --> 00:23:11.708
of your life and wherever
you go from here.

00:23:11.708 --> 00:23:13.527
- Thank you.

